[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: workflow.BUGFIX
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[BUGFIX]
overview: Rules specific to fixing bugs — making the code do what it was supposed to do
applies_when: User reports incorrect behavior, a crash, a regression, a test failure, or an edge case that breaks
follows: PHASES.toon with extra emphasis on reproducing FIRST and addressing root cause

[RULE_1_REPRODUCE_BEFORE_FIX]
what: You must reproduce the bug deterministically before writing any fix
why: A "fix" without a reproducer has no proof it actually fixes anything. It might hide the symptom while the real bug lurks.
how:
  - In DISCOVER phase: produce a minimal reproducer — a failing test, a shell command with expected-vs-actual, or step-by-step with screenshots
  - If you cannot reproduce → STOP and ask the user for more details, do not guess
  - If reproduction requires env the agent doesn't have → say so, request the user reproduce and share output

[RULE_2_WRITE_THE_FAILING_TEST_FIRST]
what: The first code change in a bugfix is a test that fails for the right reason
why: Failing test = proof you understand the bug. Passing test after fix = proof the fix works. Without both, you're flying blind.
how:
  - In IMPLEMENT phase: add the failing test BEFORE touching the bugged code
  - Run it — confirm it fails with the expected error message
  - Only then fix the underlying code
  - After fix: re-run, confirm it passes
  - If the project's test setup makes this infeasible → say so in the plan; propose an alternative (manual reproducer script) and get approval

[RULE_3_ROOT_CAUSE_OVER_SYMPTOM]
what: Fix the actual cause, not the visible symptom
why: Symptom-fixing creates Whac-A-Mole bugs — the same cause manifests elsewhere later
how:
  - In DISCOVER phase: trace the failure back to its origin. Ask "why" at least 3 times.
  - In PLAN phase: name the root cause explicitly, not just the fix
  - If the root cause is out of scope (deep architectural flaw) → state that, propose a scoped symptomatic fix with a follow-up task, get user's explicit OK to treat the symptom

[RULE_4_WHY_DIDNT_TESTS_CATCH_IT]
what: For every bugfix, answer: why didn't existing tests catch this?
why: A bug that slipped through tests is ALSO a test-coverage bug. Fix both.
how:
  - In PLAN phase, include a short section: "Why existing tests missed this"
  - Possible answers: untested code path / assertion too loose / environment difference / race condition / missing edge case
  - The fix plan should address the test gap, not just the code gap — usually this means the failing test from RULE_2 closes the gap

[RULE_5_NO_OPPORTUNISTIC_CHANGES]
what: A bugfix commit changes only what's needed to fix the bug
why: Extra changes bundled with a bugfix make regression hunting harder and reviews slower
how:
  - In IMPLEMENT phase: resist the urge to "clean up" or "also fix" nearby code
  - If you spot adjacent issues — log them as separate tasks (spawn_task), do NOT bundle
  - A bugfix diff should be the smallest change that makes the failing test pass

[RULE_6_REGRESSION_CHECK]
what: Before declaring done, run the broader test suite in the affected area
why: A fix that introduces a new regression is net-negative — the bug count stayed the same, the debt grew
how:
  - In VERIFY phase: don't just run the new test; run neighboring tests too
  - For UI bugs: manually exercise adjacent features in the browser
  - For backend: run the module's full test file at minimum

[RULE_7_DIAGNOSIS_IS_WORTH_DOCUMENTING]
what: If the bug was subtle (race condition, encoding issue, platform-specific, off-by-one in edge case), the commit message and/or a code comment should explain WHY the fix works
why: The code change alone may look arbitrary; a future reader needs to understand the invariant being preserved
how:
  - Add a short comment ONLY when the fix is non-obvious (see CLAUDE.md "WHY not WHAT" rule)
  - The commit message carries the detailed explanation
  - Do not add comments explaining the obvious

[BUGFIX_PLAN_CHECKLIST]
Before leaving PLAN phase, confirm:
  - [ ] Deterministic reproducer exists (failing test, script, or step-by-step)
  - [ ] Root cause identified and named (not just the symptom)
  - [ ] "Why existing tests missed this" answered
  - [ ] Fix plan names the smallest change that would make the reproducer pass
  - [ ] No bundled cleanups or adjacent fixes
  - [ ] Regression-check scope named (which tests / which features to re-verify)
